  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 310 PM EDT Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Widespread cloudiness continues across most of the \narea today as low pressure continues to spin off the New Jersey \ncoast. Some lingering light rain will persist on the lower Maryland \nEastern Shore through this evening. \n\nA nicer day for most as winds have decreased and temps have \nincreased compared to the past few days. The pesky and persistent \nlow pressure system, which has been responsible for the unsettled \nweather and coastal flooding (see key message 2), remains just N of \nthe area, centered (at ~1000 mb) over southern New Jersey. The one \nexception is across the lower MD Eastern Shore where continued waves \nof light rain and drizzle continue to permeate, which is also \nkeeping the temps around 60 degrees. Elsewhere, temps have generally \nwarmed into the 70s under rather widespread cloudiness. \n\nExpect some decrease in cloud cover tonight as the low moves back \noffshore and drifts NE toward coastal New England. However, thicker \nclouds will still remain prevalent across northern portions of our \narea. Lows range from the mid 50s to around 60 F. Winds will also \nfurther subside as the pressure gradient relaxes further, with just \nsome mild gustiness persisting along the immediate coast of the \nEastern Shore. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A warming trend trend will bring above average high \ntemperatures in the mid to upper 80s by late week, along with \ndry/pleasant conditions. \n\nThe low pressure system should be a shell of its former self by \nlater Monday and Tuesday, with just a weak sfc reflection and \nlimited moisture as it slowly traverses the nearshore waters off of \nthe Canadian Maritimes. Locally, a rather substantial warming trend \nbegins Monday and continues through the end of the week. Highs \nMonday range from the upper 60s/around 70 F on the Eastern Shore to \nthe mid-upper 70s for mainland VA and NC. Highs Tuesday begin to \nflirt with the 80s again, followed by widespread 80s Wednesday and \npotentially near 90 F Thursday and Friday. Several degrees cooler \neach day on the Eastern Shore. The sfc and upper-air synoptics \nsupport dry wx through most of the work week. By late Friday into \nthe weekend, some sort of southern stream or elongated frontal \nsystem could bring precip chances back to the region. However, there \nis poor agreement across deterministic and ensemble guidance on the \nevolution of any potential system; thus, PoPs are only 20-40% from \nthe blended guidance. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Coastal flooding impacts will gradually wrap up \nacross the lower Chesapeake Bay and James/York Rivers today. A few \nmore rounds of mainly minor flooding is possible with the upcoming \nhigh tides in the upper bay through tomorrow.\n\nAfter numerous days of moderate to major tidal flooding, it appears \nthat the last significant tide of this event has occurred or will \noccur by late this afternoon. Low pressure near the NJ coast will \nmove NE while weakening. This will allow the N-NW winds to decrease \nrather rapidly this evening into the overnight hours. It will take \nsome time for the water to move out of the Chesapeake Bay, but it \nshould gradually do so due to strong ebb tides at the mouth of \nChesapeake Bay expected per CBOFS. As such, will allow all coastal \nflood warnings to expire later today. A few locations will need \nadvisories tonight to cover residual minor flooding in the middle \nChesapeake Bay due to the elevated anomalies and continued higher \nastronomical tides in association with the lunar cycle, but \nmoderate flooding is not expected overnight.",
